      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes a lot of sense for training purposes. Tying the specific threat profiles directly to a tactical posture is exactly what we need to help officers reduce hesitation in high-risk moments or prevent over-escalation in lower-risk ones.\n\nLooking at the \"African American\" entry in particular, the pairing of \"Active/Physical\" resistance with \"High-readiness/Hands-on\" posture is a critical piece of safety training. To make this even more effective for the field, can you expand on the \"why\" behind these pairings? \n\nCould you provide a brief professional justification for why these specific criminal concerns and resistance types are most statistically or culturally prevalent in those demographic groups? Having that \"rationale\" in the manual helps officers trust the training and understand the situational awareness they're being taught. For example, why would an officer expect \"Active/Physical\" resistance more frequently in one group versus another?",
